Title:
NtWarden — Windows Analysis and Research Toolkit

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
NtWarden is a Windows system inspection and research toolkit with an ImGui + DirectX 11 GUI, supporting both local analysis and remote inspection via a companion TCP server (WinSysServer). It provides user-mode visibility (processes, services, network connections, ETW sessions, IPC, Object Manager, registry) and optional kernel-mode visibility through a test-signed kernel driver (KWinSys). Kernel features focus on integrity and tamper detection, including callback enumeration/integrity checks, SSDT hook detection, kernel module inventory, minifilters, WFP callouts, and kernel .text verification against disk. A per-process “Analyze Process” workflow checks for common in-memory tradecraft indicators such as unbacked executable memory, hollowing heuristics, module stomping, direct syscalls outside ntdll, and userland inline hooks (Capstone-assisted). It’s useful for red teamers and malware analysts to validate stealth techniques and for blue team/DFIR to triage suspicious processes and kernel tampering in lab environments. Remote protocol has no authentication, so it’s intended for isolated VMs/test networks.

Technical Focus:
- Windows user-mode and kernel-mode enumeration (processes/services/network/objects)
- Kernel telemetry: callbacks, SSDT, IRP dispatch tables, minifilters, WFP
- Hook/tamper detection (userland inline hooks, syscall stub integrity, kernel .text integrity)
- Memory forensics heuristics (unbacked RX pages, hollowing, module stomping)
- Kernel driver research tooling (EPROCESS enumeration, pool allocations, descriptor tables)
- Remote inspection over custom binary TCP protocol

Use Cases:
- Investigate suspicious processes for injection/hollowing/stomping indicators
- Enumerate and validate kernel callbacks/SSDT/IRP hooks during rootkit or EDR research
- Compare user-mode syscall stubs and detect inline hooks in common DLLs (ntdll/kernel32)
- Remote triage of Windows VMs via WinSysServer (lab-only)
- Inspect WFP callouts/filters and minifilter stacks for network/file-system monitoring components
- Validate DSE/CI policy state and kernel integrity assumptions in test environments

Keywords:
Windows internals, ImGui, DirectX 11, kernel driver, WDK, EPROCESS, SSDT, kernel callbacks, IRP dispatch table, ETW sessions, Object Manager, named pipes, RPC endpoints, WFP callouts, minifilter, Code Integrity, DSE, module stomping, process hollowing, direct syscalls, ntdll hooks, kernel .text integrity, EPT hook detection, pool tags, TCP remote inspection, custom binary protocol